// A report with grid layout: `## artifact { layout=grid tiles=[…] }` at model
// level, one tile per query of the report's own, laid out by Publisher into the grid
// `dashboard { columns=N }` names. The tag makes this file a report and
// `layout=` decides how it is laid out; where the file lives decides nothing,
// so it sits at the package root beside the model it reads. The same tiles
// read top to bottom are overview-story.malloy, a linear report.
//
// The tag has to be model-level because a composite has no query of its own to
// hang a `#` tag on, and it is model-level for a second reason worth knowing:
// tiles run as separate queries, which is the only way a report can span
// unrelated sources. A nest's pipeline starts from its own query's source, so no
// single Malloy result does.
//
// The filter controls are declared HERE, in the report, and each tile says
// which of them it reads. That is the convention for a report the builder
// edits: the builder adds and removes filters, and a filter it can add has to be
// a declaration in the one file it writes. It never edits imports or the model.
//
// It is also the only way the binding works, and worth knowing before moving a
// declaration: binding is per DECLARATION, not per name. A given declared here
// does not drive a `where:` written in the model, and a `where:` written here
// cannot read a given from givens.malloy. So the filtering lives on the tiles,
// below, each naming the controls it answers to.
//
// Compare `order_items -> business_overview` in storefront.malloy, which is the
// same figures as one query with nests. That form is Malloy's own rendering
// feature and still works; this is the form to author a report in.
##! experimental.givens

// The page's narrative header. Model-level doc comments (`##"`) become the
// report's description, and Publisher renders that as MARKDOWN: paragraphs,
// emphasis, lists, links and inline code all work, and a bare `##"` line
// separates paragraphs.
//
// It has to be `##"` and not `#"`. A doc comment attaches to an object, and at
// model level there is none to attach to, so `#"` here fails the package load
// with "Object annotation not connected to any object". The single-query form
// is the other way round: its `#"` sits on the `query:` and attaches to that.
//
// This is the page-level prose, and it is per PAGE. Prose next to one tile is
// a `# subtitle="…"` on that tile's view, one line. Prose BETWEEN tiles is a
// text tile: a `kind=text` entry in `tiles=[…]` plus a `##|(markdown) name` block.
##" Revenue, margin and volume for the order lines the controls select.
##"
##" **Key figures** is the summary; everything under it explains the summary.
##" The monthly trend says which way the numbers are moving, and the state,
##" category and product breakdowns say where they came from.
##"
##" Every tile re-runs when a control changes, so narrowing to a single
##" `Category` or `Region` reframes the whole page rather than filtering one
##" list in it.
## artifact { layout=grid title="Storefront overview" tiles=["kpis" { colspan=12 }, "revenue_trend" { colspan=6 break }, "revenue_by_state" { colspan=6 }, "revenue_by_category" { colspan=6 break }, "best_sellers" { colspan=6 }] } dashboard { columns=12 }
// What this page reads from the model, by name. `order_items` is what every
// tile runs over. `products`, `regions` and `brand_suggest` are what the
// controls' pickers read their options from, and a `suggest { source= }` or
// `{ query= }` has to resolve in THIS file: an import is not transitive, so
// `brand_suggest` needs `order_items` beside it too, or the Brand picker answers
// 400 "Undefined source 'order_items'" and offers no options. The load lint
// names a missing suggest source; it cannot name this one.
import {
   order_items,
   products,
   regions,
   brand_suggest
} from "storefront.malloy"

// The controls. A given is a typed runtime parameter (docs/givens.md), and the
// tags on each declaration are its control contract: what the input is
// labelled, which widget renders it, and where a picker gets its choices.
// Declaration order is control order.
//
// Only a given some tile READS becomes a control, so a declaration nothing
// binds costs nothing and shows nothing. Which tiles a control drives is on the
// tiles themselves, below; here is only what each control is.
# description="Narrow to one product category. Leave empty for all"
# label="Category" control=select suggest { source=products dimension=category }
given: CATEGORY :: filter<string> is f''

# description="One or more brands. Leave empty for all"
# label="Brand" control=multiselect suggest { query=brand_suggest dimension=brand }
given: BRAND :: filter<string> is f''

# description="Narrow to one US sales region. Leave empty for all"
# label="Region" control=select suggest { source=regions dimension=region }
given: REGION :: filter<string> is f''

// A plain (non-filter) `date` renders as a date picker and binds with `>=`,
// not `~`: it is a value, not a filter expression. The storefront data runs
// 2023-01-01 through 2025-12-31, so this default takes in all of it.
# description="Only count orders placed on or after this date"
# label="Ordered since"
given: SINCE :: date is @2023-01-01

// Numeric bounds turn a `filter<number>` into a slider instead of a text box.
# description="Ignore line items below this sale price"
# label="Minimum line total" range_min=0 range_max=250
given: MIN_SALE :: filter<number> is f''

// The tiles. Each is a query of this report's own over a view the model serves:
// the view keeps its chart and shape in storefront.malloy, and the query adds
// what this page says about it, its title as a `#` tag above it and its control
// bindings as a `+ { where: … }`. The report declares no source of its own, so
// nothing that lists the package's sources sees a second copy of order_items.
//
// Where each tile sits is on its entry in `tiles=[…]` above (`colspan`, `break`),
// not on the query, so moving or resizing a tile changes the list and nothing
// else. This is the form the Console builder writes.
//
// Each tile's `+ { where: … }` is its bindings: one clause per control it
// answers to. Every tile here answers to all five, which is what makes a
// control move the whole page; a tile that left one out would simply not move
// when that control did.

// A full-width KPI strip. `# big_value` renders each aggregate as a figure
// rather than a table row. The figures are `key_figures` in storefront.malloy.
# label="Key figures"
# big_value
query: kpis is order_items -> key_figures + { where: category ~ $CATEGORY, where: brand ~ $BRAND, where: region ~ $REGION, where: created_at >= $SINCE, where: sale_price ~ $MIN_SALE }

// `break` on its entry starts a fresh row, so the trend leads the second row
// however the strip above it wraps.
//
// Six and six rather than eight and four, and the map is why. A root
// `# shape_map` is drawn at a size of its own (@malloydata/render builds its
// spec from a hardcoded 500x350, 512px wide with the legend below it), so it
// does not shrink into a narrower tile, it is clipped by one. Six of twelve is
// about 631px here and fits; the map clips again in a grid under about 1070px.
# label="Revenue by month"
query: revenue_trend is order_items -> sales_by_month + { where: category ~ $CATEGORY, where: brand ~ $BRAND, where: region ~ $REGION, where: created_at >= $SINCE, where: sale_price ~ $MIN_SALE }

# label="Revenue by state"
query: revenue_by_state is order_items -> sales_by_state + { where: category ~ $CATEGORY, where: brand ~ $BRAND, where: region ~ $REGION, where: created_at >= $SINCE, where: sale_price ~ $MIN_SALE }

# label="Revenue by category"
query: revenue_by_category is order_items -> by_category + { where: category ~ $CATEGORY, where: brand ~ $BRAND, where: region ~ $REGION, where: created_at >= $SINCE, where: sale_price ~ $MIN_SALE }

# label="Top products"
query: best_sellers is order_items -> top_products + { where: category ~ $CATEGORY, where: brand ~ $BRAND, where: region ~ $REGION, where: created_at >= $SINCE, where: sale_price ~ $MIN_SALE }
